Spinor /: Spinor::usage = "Spinor[p, m, o] is the head of Dirac spinors. \
Which of the spinors $u$, $v$,\n$\\bar{u}$ or $\\bar{v}$ is understood, \
depends on the sign of the momentum\nargument p and the relative position of \
Spinor in the chain.\n\n- Spinor[Momentum[p], m] means $\\bar{u}$ if it \
stands at the beginning of the\nchain.\n\n- Spinor[Momentum[p], m] means $u$ \
if it stands at the end of the chain.\n\n- Spinor[-Momentum[p], m] means \
$\\bar{v}$ if it stands at the beginning of the\nchain.\n\n- \
Spinor[-Momentum[p], m] means $v$ if it stands at the end of the \
chain.\n\nSpinors of fermions of mass $m$ are normalized to have $\\bar{u} \
u=2 m$ and\n$\\bar{v} v=-2 m$.\n\nThe optional argument o can be used for \
additional degrees of freedom. If no\noptional argument o is supplied, a 1 is \
substituted in."
 
