FCFeynmanParameterJoin /: FCFeynmanParameterJoin::failmsg = "Error! \
FCFeynmanParameterJoin has encountered a fatal problem and must abort the \
computation. The problem reads: `1`"
 
FCFeynmanParameterJoin /: FCFeynmanParameterJoin::usage = "FCFeynmanParameter\
Join[{{{prop1,prop2,x},prop3,y},...}, {p1,p2,...}] joins all\npropagators in \
int using Feynman parameters but does not integrate over the\nloop momenta \
$p_i$. The function returns {fpInt,pref,vars}, where fpInt is the\npiece of \
the integral that contains a single GFAD-type propagator and pref is\nthe \
part containing the res. The introduced Feynman parameters are listed \
in\nvars. The overall Dirac delta is omitted.\n\nNotice that each inner list \
must contain exactly thee elements, the first two\nbeing propagators (or \
products of propagators) and the last one denoting the\nhead of Feynman \
parameter variables used to join those propagators. \
For\nexample,\n{FAD[{p1,m1}],FAD[{p2,m2}],x},  but \
also\n{FAD[{p1,m1}],FAD[{p2,m2}]FAD[{p3,m3}],x} or \
even\n{FAD[{p1,m1}]FAD[{p2,m2}],FAD[{p3,m3}]FAD[{p4,m4}],x} represent valid \
examples\nof such lists, while something like \
{FAD[{p1,m1}],FAD[{p2,m2}],FAD[{p3,m3}],x}\n(4 instead of 3 list elements) is \
invalid and will not work.\n\nHaving obtained an output of \
FCFeynmanParameterJoin (e.g. called intT), you\nshould use the following \
syntax to pass this to\nFCFeynmanParametrize:  \
FCFeynmanParametrize[intT[[1]],intT[[2]],{lmom1, \
lmom2,\n...},Variables->intT[[3]]]"
 
